Why this matters for efficiency
Reservoir computing is efficient because you avoid full end-to-end training.
You exploit a rich fixed dynamical system, then train a cheap readout.
That matches our efficiency argument perfectly:
do not train/reason over everything\text{do not train/reason over everything}do not train/reason over everything
use the model as a rich feature generator\text{use the model as a rich feature generator}use the model as a rich feature generator
use grammar/graph to cheaply classify and control\text{use grammar/graph to cheaply classify and control}use grammar/graph to cheaply classify and control
So the efficient version is:
1. Freeze the LLM or encoder.2. Use it as a high-dimensional semantic reservoir.3. Extract typed scores / embeddings.4. Train a small classifier/readout.5. Enforce graph transitions deterministically.6. Use singularity/IDF signals to decide when to spend more compute.
Compact answer
Yes, if the model and graph dynamics are mostly fixed and only the readout/controller is trained, then this is reservoir computing in spirit.
But the full architecture is more than classical reservoir computing:
reservoir computing+typed grammar+graph state machine+hyperbolic geometry+singularity-aware readout\boxed{
